%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Docs",
    title: "Getting Started",
    summary: "Create a Klenod build context, collect an entry, import dependencies, build a bundle, and load it with the runtime gem."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Create A Context" }
    :markdown
      A build context starts with a source directory. `context.entry(...)` collects the requested module and returns a reusable handle without evaluating app code.

      ```ruby
      context = Klenod::Build::Context.new(source_dir: "src")
      entry = context.entry("pages/server")

      exports = entry.exports
      status, headers, body = entry.call(request, context)
      ```

      Use `entry.exports` or `entry.call(...)` when you want the module to evaluate. The same entry handle can be kept across development updates.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Import Files" }
    :markdown
      Ruby modules can import other modules with literal `import("...")` calls. Relative imports resolve from the importer, and leading slash imports resolve from the configured source root.

      ```ruby
      Shared = import("../shared")
      Styles = import("/styles/home.css")
      Hero = import("./hero.png?width=320,640&format=webp")
      Article = import("./article.md")
      ```

  %DocsSection{ title: "Configure Builds" }
    :markdown
      The CLI reads `klenod.config.rb`, changes into the config directory, and builds from there.

      ```ruby
      source_dir "src"
      entrypoint "pages/server"
      output "dist/klenod.bundle"
      assets_dir "public"
      mode :development
      ```

      ```sh
      bundle exec klenod build
      ```

  %DocsSection{ title: "Load Runtime Bundles" }
    :markdown
      Production code that only loads a prebuilt bundle should depend on `klenod-runtime`. It does not need build-only dependencies such as Haml, CSS, or image transformers.

      ```ruby
      require "klenod/runtime"

      bundle = Klenod::Runtime.load_bundle("dist/klenod.bundle")
      server = bundle.exports("pages/server")
      ```
